# ---- GOLDEN FIXTURE (bridge tile) ----
# resetMode=loadvm -> restore the INTERNAL 'golden' snapshot: X (-nocursor) +
# MAME 0.289 `orica -bios ver11` at the Oric Atmos's own untouched power-on
# screen. Nothing is curated into the fixture and nothing is typed after the
# restore. The builder's keyboard proof deliberately runs AFTER the bake, so no
# verification output can ever end up inside the golden (the mpf2 lesson).
SH_RESET_MODE=loadvm
SH_GOLDEN_SNAPSHOT=golden
SH_FIXTURE_DESC=Oric Atmos (1984) at its own untouched power-on screen: "ORIC EXTENDED BASIC V1.1 / (c) 1983 TANGERINE / 37631 BYTES FREE / Ready" in black on a white page, with the machine's own CAPS marker in the top-right corner, drawn fullscreen with aspect correction by MAME 0.289's `orica` driver on an 800x600 X root — 4:3, the shape the Atmos drew on a television, and 1.6x cheaper to blit than 1024x768 on this box. Keyboard-only exhibit; no pointer device; AY-3-8912 sound -> ALSA -> AC97.

# ---- KEYBOARD PACING (the exhibit's only input surface) ----
# MAME samples the emulated keyboard matrix once per emulated frame. The Atmos
# runs at 50.080128 Hz (19.97 ms), so the frame-derived two-frame floor is
# 40/40. Bisected on this tile 2026-08-09 (40-character line, 10 trials per
# rung, frames compared against a known-good complete render because the
# blinking cursor costs exactly one 1260-byte cell and a dropped character
# costs ~3000):
#
#   hold/gap   lines corrupted
#     40/40    0 of 10
#     60/60    0 of 10
#     80/80    0 of 10
#    250/250   the harness's own "undisputed" reference DROPPED 7 of 40
#
# So on this machine a LONG hold is the failure mode, not a short one, and the
# frame-derived floor already works. 80/80 ships anyway: it is what every other
# 50 Hz tile in the fleet uses, it is four frames of margin against host
# scheduling, and it measured no worse. The 105-character registry demoProgram
# types intact at these values (evidence/demo-typein-listing.png).
SH_KEY_MIN_HOLD_MS=80
SH_KEY_MIN_GAP_MS=80
# X AUTO-REPEAT IS OFF IN THE KIOSK, and that is load-bearing rather than
# cosmetic. Every key here is a SYNTHETIC press/release pair; when the release
# is delivered late, X's typematic repeat floods the guest with the held key.
# Measured before the fix: the demo listing's line 40 arrived as
# PRINT "ORIC ATMOS 19999999999 -- one late release, eleven nines -- and the
# stuck key left the machine deaf to everything typed afterwards. With
# `xset r off` in /etc/bridge/launch.sh the same listing arrives byte-perfect.
# The golden was re-baked after that change; the setting lives inside it.
#
# No SH_KEY_MAP. MAME maps the host's PC keys onto the Oric matrix by position
# and the Atmos's layout is ASCII-shaped, so the SPA's US set1 scancodes arrive
# as the characters they print. The machine boots in CAPS (it says so in the
# top-right corner), so typed lower case reaches BASIC as upper case.
#
# MAME's own UI keys are OFF because `orica` emulates a full 59-key keyboard:
# a visitor pressing Tab gets the Oric's key, not MAME's menu. No UI lockout is
# needed here, unlike a partial-keyboard arcade driver.
